Label $3$ points $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ on the interior of the hexagon such that $AB=BC=4.\ \hint{How does that make $\triangle{ABC}$ show up in the diagram above?}$. Draw the altitude from the midpoint of ${AC}$. Finding the point $5$ units right of $B$, $D$, makes $\triangle{BCD}$ the desired triangle. We compute its side lengths using the Pythagorean Theorem. [asy]
pair A, B, C, D, E, F;
A=(0,0);
B=(0,1);
C=(1,1);
E=(-0.5,1);
D=(1.5,1);
F=(1,1.5);
draw(A--B--C--cycle);
draw(B--D);
draw(A--E);
draw(C--F);
draw(B--E,linetype("4 4"));
draw(B--F,linetype("4 4"));
label("1",midpoint(A--B),NW);
label("4",midpoint(C--F),NE);
label("5",midpoint(B--D),SE);
dot(A);
dot(B);
dot(C);
dot(D);
dot(E);
dot(F);
label("$A$",A,W);
label("$B$",B,NW);
label("$C$",C,NE);
label("$D$",D,NE);
label("$E$",E,W);
label("$F$",F,N);[/asy] The desired area is half that of the equilateral $\triangle{BCD}$, or $\dfrac\sqrt34(5^2)$. <answer> $\dfrac{25}4\sqrt3$</answer>

